WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:18.769
March 28 Dear Daddy Longlegs, So many good things have happened!

2
00:00:20.229 --> 00:00:24.090
I won the short story contest that the college magazine holds every year.

3
00:00:25.531 --> 00:00:26.711
And I'm just a sophomore!

4
00:00:27.871 --> 00:00:29.952
The other contestants were mostly seniors.

5
00:00:32.876 --> 00:00:37.059
When I saw my name posted, I couldn't believe I'd won.

6
00:00:39.421 --> 00:00:41.582
First prize is twenty-five dollars.

7
00:00:43.424 --> 00:00:46.286
I also got a part in the spring play, As You Like It.

8
00:00:47.286 --> 00:00:48.847
That's something else that Shakespeare wrote.

9
00:00:49.608 --> 00:00:50.949
Besides Hamlet, of course.

10
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:57.814
Last but not least Julia, Sallie, and I are traveling to New York City next weekend.

11
00:00:59.115 --> 00:01:00.936
We're going to do some spring shopping.

12
00:01:02.052 --> 00:01:04.994
Master Jervie has invited us to the theater on Saturday.

13
00:01:06.215 --> 00:01:08.236
Julia will stay at home with her family.

14
00:01:09.156 --> 00:01:11.338
But Sallie and I are going to stay at a hotel.

15
00:01:12.738 --> 00:01:15.280
I've never been in a hotel or a theater!

16
00:01:16.621 --> 00:01:18.622
And what do you think we're going to see at the theater?

17
00:01:19.843 --> 00:01:21.564
Hamlet. Imagine that!

18
00:01:23.745 --> 00:01:26.026
We studied it for a month in Shakespeare class.

19
00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:28.828
I know it by heart.

20
00:01:30.716 --> 00:01:33.078
I'm so excited that I can hardly sleep.

21
00:01:34.438 --> 00:01:42.163
Yours, Judy April 7 Dear Daddy Longlegs, My goodness!

22
00:01:42.643 --> 00:01:43.924
New York City is big!

23
00:01:46.645 --> 00:01:48.846
Do you really live in all that confusion?

24
00:01:51.848 --> 00:01:54.890
It'll take me months to recover from just two days there.

25
00:01:56.151 --> 00:01:58.332
I can't begin to tell you everything I saw.

26
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.601
But I guess you know what New York is like.

27
00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:06.783
Sallie, Julia, and I went shopping on Saturday morning.

28
00:02:08.143 --> 00:02:12.265
Those shop windows were filled with so many lovely things.

29
00:02:13.546 --> 00:02:15.767
They make you want to devote your life to clothes.

30
00:02:15.787 --> 00:02:19.628
"I need some new hats," Julia said.

31
00:02:21.409 --> 00:02:24.470
We entered the most gorgeous shop on the block.

32
00:02:26.071 --> 00:02:27.992
The walls were white and gold.

33
00:02:29.272 --> 00:02:32.055
There were blue silk curtains and gold chairs.

34
00:02:34.397 --> 00:02:37.299
A pretty blond woman in a black gown welcomed us.

35
00:02:38.601 --> 00:02:41.223
It was like paying a social call at someone's home!

36
00:02:41.243 --> 00:02:45.887
"Please take a seat in front of the mirror," she said to Julia.

37
00:02:47.429 --> 00:02:50.631
Then the woman brought over a selection of beautiful hats.

38
00:02:50.651 --> 00:02:55.296
"Each one is lovelier than the last," Sallie said.

39
00:02:59.752 --> 00:03:02.514
I could only nod and watch as Julia tried them on.

40
00:03:06.316 --> 00:03:09.818
At last Julia pointed to the two loveliest hats of all.

41
00:03:09.838 --> 00:03:12.939
"I'll take both of those."

42
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.702
She handed the woman a card with her name and address.

43
00:03:16.722 --> 00:03:19.403
"Please send them to this address."

44
00:03:19.423 --> 00:03:23.305
"Julia never asked the prices!"

45
00:03:23.826 --> 00:03:24.846
Sallie whispered to me.

46
00:03:24.866 --> 00:03:26.827
"I know!"

47
00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:27.988
I whispered back.

48
00:03:29.084 --> 00:03:32.627
"I cannot imagine being able to buy any hat I wanted!"

49
00:03:34.568 --> 00:03:37.150
Next we met Master Jervie for lunch at Sherry's.

50
00:03:38.691 --> 00:03:39.672
Have you ever been there?

51
00:03:41.013 --> 00:03:44.015
Picture Sherry's—it's a really fancy restaurant.

52
00:03:45.216 --> 00:03:49.779
Then picture the dining room at the orphanage with its plain white dishes and worn-out tables.

53
00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.381
Quite the contrast!

54
00:03:53.962 --> 00:03:55.784
I ate my fish with the wrong fork.

55
00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.241
But the waiter very kindly gave me another one.

56
00:04:01.302 --> 00:04:02.462
I don't think anyone noticed.

57
00:04:04.963 --> 00:04:07.224
After lunch we went straight to the theater.

58
00:04:09.265 --> 00:04:11.146
I sat between Sallie and Master Jervie.

59
00:04:11.166 --> 00:04:13.407
"Are you eager to see the play?"

60
00:04:13.747 --> 00:04:14.287
he asked me.

61
00:04:14.307 --> 00:04:15.908
"Oh yes!

62
00:04:16.328 --> 00:04:17.689
I've been looking forward to it!"

63
00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:22.631
I was so excited, and the play hadn't even started yet.

64
00:04:24.411 --> 00:04:25.832
Hamlet was amazing!

65
00:04:26.272 --> 00:04:27.633
Dazzling! Marvelous!

66
00:04:27.653 --> 00:04:31.196
"Isn't Shakespeare wonderful?"

67
00:04:31.576 --> 00:04:33.398
Master Jervie said to me at intermission.

68
00:04:33.418 --> 00:04:41.405
"I thought I liked Hamlet while I was reading it, but now It's so much better onstage than

69
00:04:41.505 --> 00:04:42.465
reading it at school."

70
00:04:43.546 --> 00:04:44.767
Master Jervie smiled.

71
00:04:45.948 --> 00:04:48.010
I'm sure my eyes were wide as saucers.

72
00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:49.151
But I don't care.

73
00:04:50.212 --> 00:04:52.474
Maybe I'll become an actress instead of a writer.

74
00:04:53.394 --> 00:04:55.136
I'll send you tickets for all my shows.

75
00:04:56.948 --> 00:04:58.849
Then I'll smile at you from the stage.

76
00:05:00.129 --> 00:05:02.310
Please wear a red rose in your buttonhole.

77
00:05:03.211 --> 00:05:04.751
I'd hate to smile at the wrong man.

78
00:05:06.832 --> 00:05:08.613
We returned to school on Saturday night.

79
00:05:09.853 --> 00:05:11.314
We ate supper on the train.

80
00:05:11.674 --> 00:05:13.435
There were little tables with pink lamps.

81
00:05:13.455 --> 00:05:20.738
"I never heard of meals being served on a train," I said as we sat down.

82
00:05:20.758 --> 00:05:24.839
"Where on earth did you grow up?"

83
00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:25.980
Julia asked.

84
00:05:27.628 --> 00:05:29.390
"In a village," I said quietly.

85
00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:32.632
I hoped she wouldn't ask any more questions.

86
00:05:32.652 --> 00:05:35.315
"Did you ever travel?"

87
00:05:35.735 --> 00:05:36.255
she asked.

88
00:05:37.536 --> 00:05:38.737
"Not till I went to college.

89
00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:44.082
And then it was only 160 miles, and we didn't eat in the dining car."

90
00:05:46.124 --> 00:05:46.764
She smiled.

91
00:05:46.784 --> 00:05:48.946
"I can't imagine that.

92
00:05:50.447 --> 00:05:52.049
You say the funniest things!"

93
00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:58.001
I try not to say funny things, but they pop out when I'm surprised.

94
00:05:59.342 --> 00:06:01.864
At least I don't make as many mistakes as I used to.

95
00:06:03.465 --> 00:06:06.307
I'm learning how people who didn't grow up in orphanages live.

96
00:06:07.828 --> 00:06:10.149
So I feel more comfortable around the other girls now.

97
00:06:12.071 --> 00:06:13.832
I forgot to tell you about our flowers!

98
00:06:16.394 --> 00:06:20.536
Master Jervie gave us each a bunch of violets and lilies of the valley.

99
00:06:21.877 --> 00:06:23.098
Wasn't that sweet of him?

100
00:06:23.758 --> 00:06:25.640
I never used to care much for men.

101
00:06:26.989 --> 00:06:28.674
But I'm changing my mind.

102
00:06:31.142 --> 00:06:32.105
Yours always, Judy
